#{[#edb/ref 17592186048189 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065076} :activity/id #uuid "0dc1fb7b-6336-57cd-b507-8bd6ddfc25fc" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065076 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065433 :tutorial/content "Consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral, apart from the coefficient of $4$ in the denominator, resembles the basic integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin(u)+C.\n$$\n\nIn fact, they look almost the same, but instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $4x^2 = (2x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can often use this trick whenever we see an integral that closely resembles an integral for an inverse trigonometric function.\n\n**Note:** As always, after solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)+C] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065077}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064218 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064090 :question/id #uuid "48ec6930-88b7-5aad-9bb7-c5e23e766f80" :question/math-academy-id "e-470" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $9x^2 = (3x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\,\\textrm d u=\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(3x)^{2}}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡3x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064219 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064091 :question/id #uuid "3e89a278-54ab-59a6-adb6-38f43c0fbe9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-471" :question/problem "Calculate the integral  $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x }.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int \\dfrac{3}{2(1 + 25x^2)} = \\dfrac 3 2 \\int \\dfrac{1}{1+25x^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^2}\\textrm{d}u =\\arctan u +C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $25x^2 = (5x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 5\\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{2+50{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(5x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡5x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065124}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064220 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064092 :question/id #uuid "cb771ff3-b5a9-5900-913f-df23957760b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-472" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt 2} \\dfrac{1}{2+x^2}\\, \\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2(1+{x}^{2}/2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2(1+(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this integral now resembles the basic integral for the inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{1}{1+u^2}\\,\\textrm d u = \\arctan{u} + C.\n$$\n\nWe make the substitution $u=\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt 2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\sqrt 2 \\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065182}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065124 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064250 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064101 :question/id #uuid "56860611-869d-5035-bf8f-f568c774d78d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5014" :question/problem "${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{5\\, \\textrm{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^2-1}}  = }$" :question/worked-solution "This resembles the basic integral for $\\textrm{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^2-1}} \\textrm{d}u = \\textrm{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nLet $u = 5x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n5\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5\\,\\text{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^{2}-1}} & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(u)+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(5x)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065182 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065381 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we learned how to use substitution to evaluate integrals that give rise to inverse trigonometric functions.\n\nAlthough you can always solve these integrals by going through the substitution process, it is convenient to remember the following key results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(ax)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(ax)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{a}^{2}-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{a})+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+(ax)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(ax)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{a}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a})+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{|ax|\\sqrt{(ax)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\text{arcsec}(ax)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remember the results above, note the following:\n\n- Whenever the integrand has an $ax$ term, the argument of the resulting inverse trigonometric function is $ax.$\n\n- Otherwise, if the $a$ and $x$ are separated, then the argument of the resulting inverse trigonometric function is $\\dfrac{x}{a}.$\n\n- All the results are multiplied by $\\dfrac{1}{a},$ except for $\\arcsin \\left( \\dfrac{x}{a} \\right) + C.$" :tutorial/title "Summary of Key Results"}}] :activity/title "Integration by Substitution With Inverse Trigonometric Functions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064411}] [#edb/ref 17592186049332 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093122} :activity/id #uuid "4d6a31ac-41c5-5814-8574-33c8d7e8a0e1" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093122 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093370 :tutorial/content "The chain rule can be used to differentiate logarithmic functions like \n\n$$\ny = \\ln (2x+1).\n$$\n\nFirst notice that this function can be written as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\ln u, \\qquad u = 2x+1.\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}u)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u=2x+1,$ we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{2x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093123}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093123 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092264 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092120 :question/id #uuid "5d2c0fff-c9af-5e8a-846a-640878f08283" :question/math-academy-id "e-730" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x) = \\ln(x^2-4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = \\ln u, \\qquad u = x^2-4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{u},\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = 2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Natural Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093124}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093124 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092265 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092122 :question/id #uuid "7df7fed3-c172-5cc2-aca8-abd31a0902a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-731" :question/problem "If ![](images/6b/6b4554ae23fd7bb02a3dbeda7d55c342b4f30f07b57133739709048d5bfce44a.png) what is ![](images/1e/1e6f1dc22671990b694ea0386dcdcdd843ea819101a3be99a6c5378d10c5c993.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we express ![](images/52/523ba398cc130edb20b50aa7b9a5deab0a53e4222824e3deda7f1a13ac7b0a56.png) as the composite function\n\n![](images/7d/7dc97a419c76db6b88c0862e2e54d20062ca6501657bc260eb1c478a5ad1b9eb.png)\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n![](images/c2/c27f0454bd599e68525a774e3e0989a7ee7da4eb82701d6e918ffa334c615916.png)\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n![](images/70/70aaf32dd29ad000b81dcff6e4b155fe6358e923d4d1f485f71aff511233c15b.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}}] :activity/title "The Chain Rule With Logarithmic Functions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092417}] [#edb/ref 17592186048842 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097430} :activity/id #uuid "a699a755-f56a-5a6a-8493-60b83f42eaa5" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097430 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097820 :tutorial/content "We know how to calculate the derivative of the product of two functions, but what if we have a quotient?\n\nFor that, we can use the **quotient rule**. The quotient rule is a formula to calculate the derivative of the ratio of two functions like \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac {u(x)}{v(x)}.\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule for differentiation states that the derivative of the function above is given by\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac {u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x) \\cdot v'(x)} {[v(x)]^2}.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that we have the function $f(x)$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{x}{x^2+1},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $f'(x).$ \n\nHere $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=x^2+1.$  Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{'}(x) & =(x)^{'}=1, \\\\ {v}^{'}(x) & =({x}^{2}+1)^{'}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug them in the formula, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-x\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097431}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097431 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096620 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096420 :question/id #uuid "932c9b44-c040-5ae1-84f7-277767c255d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3637" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac{x} {9x^2 + 5x},$ find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The numerator and denominator are $u(x) = x$ and $v(x) = 9x^2 + 5x,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =1, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =18x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\tf'(x) \t& = \\dfrac {u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x) v'(x)} {[v(x)]^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t& = \\dfrac{\\left( 9x^2+5x \\right )\\cdot 1 -x\\cdot \\left( 18x+5 \\right)}{\\left( 9x^2+5x\\right)^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t& = \\dfrac{\\left( 9x^2+5x-18x^2-5x \\right)}{\\left( 9x^2+5x\\right)^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t& = -\\dfrac{9x^2}{\\left( 9x^2+5x\\right)^2}.\n\t\t}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient of Two Polynomials"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097432}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097432 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096621 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096421 :question/id #uuid "f804809a-22bf-5ab3-9775-3591d4581797" :question/math-academy-id "e-3638" :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\dfrac{1+\\sin x}{x},$ find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\dfrac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x) = 1+\\sin x$ and $v(x) = x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nf'(x) & = \\dfrac{u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x)\\cdot v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2}\\\n\t\t         \t& = \\dfrac{(1+\\sin x)'\\cdot (x) -(1+\\sin x)\\cdot (x)'}{x^2}\\\n\t\t\t        & = \\dfrac{\\cos x \\cdot x- (1+\\sin x)\\cdot 1}{x^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t& = \\dfrac{x \\cos x -\\sin x -1}{x^2}\t\t\t\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097433}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097433 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097821 :tutorial/content "Using Leibniz notation, the quotient rule is expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left (\\dfrac u v\\right) = \\dfrac {v \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}u} {\\textrm{d}x}\n- u \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}}{v^2}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Leibniz Notation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097434}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097434 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096622 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096422 :question/id #uuid "53d435ea-a20f-5216-8020-f36cdf8b7c36" :question/math-academy-id "e-3639" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\dfrac{1}{3x+7},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=1$ and $v=3x+7.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\t& = \\dfrac{ v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}-u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}}{v^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t& = \\dfrac{ \\left( 3x+7 \\right ) \\cdot 0 -1\\cdot 3}{\\left( 3x+7\\right)^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t& = -\\dfrac{3}{\\left( 3x+7\\right)^2}.\n\t\t}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Using Leibniz Notation"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097435}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097435 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097822 :tutorial/content "We can now prove that $(\\tan x)' = \\sec^2{x}$ using the quotient rule. Here's how! \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}x)-(\\operatorname{sin}x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}x)^{'}}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}x)-(\\operatorname{sin}x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}x)}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}x+{sin}^{2}x}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Justifying the Formula for the Derivative of Tangent"}}] :activity/title "The Quotient Rule for Differentiation" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096890}] [#edb/ref 17592186048332 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101673} :activity/id #uuid "6d230526-c4b2-501d-9f6c-bd0d45bc17a8" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101673 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102027 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_1^2 (x-1)^5\\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Can we solve it using the method of integration by substitution?\n\nThe answer is yes, and the procedure is very similar to the one that we followed for the indefinite integrals. The main difference is that this time, we need to change the limits of integration too. We proceed via three steps, as follows.\n\n** Step 1**: Change of variable in the integrand. \n\nWe start by setting the base of the power equal to $u.$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting, we get\n\n$$\n(x-1)^5\\,\\textrm{d}x=u^5\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\n** Step 2**: Calculate the new limits. \n\nWe now have to change the limits of integration, $x=1$ and $x=2.$ Since we want to express the integral in terms of the new variable, $u,$ we need to change the variable in the limits too.\n\nThe lower limit $x=1$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x-1 \\\\ u & =1-1 \\\\ u & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile the upper limit $x=2$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x-1 \\\\ u & =(2)-1 \\\\ u & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is useful to use a table like the following when changing limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 1 & 2 \\\\ u & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Solve the integral in the new variable $u,$ using the new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-1)^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{6}{u}^{6}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1)^{6}-\\frac{1}{6}(0)^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when applying integration by substitution to definite integrals, there is no need to substitute the variable $x$ back in the final answer. We can simply evaluate it using the new variable $u.$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101682}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101682 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100874 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100704 :question/id #uuid "2359455c-4a44-5794-8b86-738bcdbbd11d" :question/math-academy-id "e-455" :question/problem "Calculate the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^0 x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\text{d}u & =2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u & =x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^2+1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & 0 \\\\ u & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^0 x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\int_2^1 u^3\\,\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2}\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}{|}_{2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{1}^{4}-{2}^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1-16}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important**: Note that in this example, when we changed the limits the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 \\ldots \\textrm{d}x$ became $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^1 \\ldots \\textrm{d}u.$ When changing the limits, it is important to respect the order in which the limits are applied. The lower limit of the integral in $u$ must correspond to the lower limit of the integral in $x.$ Same for the upper limit."} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of Function Using Substitution"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101687}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100877 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100705 :question/id #uuid "dd931400-d592-5402-b380-dee7812adac5" :question/math-academy-id "e-456" :question/problem "Calculate the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{x^2}{x^3+2}\\,\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{3}+2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u & ={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^3+2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & 2 \\\\ u & 1 & 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{1}{(x^3+2)}\\,x^2\\textrm{d}x=\\int_1^{10} \\dfrac{1}{u}\\,\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{3}\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{10}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{10}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{1}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Substitution"}}] :activity/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101028}]}
